const enBody = `<h1 id="top">Tencent Hunyuan Hy4 flash API: $0.035 per 1M Input Tokens, and a License That Changed</h1>

<p>Tencent released <strong>Hy4</strong> and <strong>Hy4 flash</strong> as open-weight models in November 2026, and both are already serving production traffic on OpenRouter. Hy4 flash is the interesting one: at <strong>$0.035 per million input tokens</strong> and <strong>$0.1405 per million output tokens</strong> it is one of the cheapest 1M-context, image-capable models on any aggregator. On the promotional rate that is <strong>65% cheaper on input</strong> than Claude Haiku 5.5, and <strong>72% cheaper on output</strong>.</p>

<p>There is a second story in this release that costs you more than the price does.</p>

<p>Hy4 preview shipped on <strong>August 28, 2026</strong> under <strong>Apache License 2.0</strong>. Hy4 and Hy4 flash, released weeks later, ship under the <strong>Tencent Hy License, Version 1.0</strong> — a modified Apache license with a <strong>Section 10</strong> that requires a separate written agreement if you operate a model-as-a-service business above <strong>$50 million</strong> in trailing 12-month revenue, or serve a product with more than <strong>100 million monthly active users</strong>. That is the clause that decides whether you can build a business on top of these weights, and almost nobody wrote about it.</p>

<p>This review prices both models against every comparable tier, checks what the weights actually cost to host, and reads the license so you do not have to.</p>

<h2 id="pricing">Pricing: two models, one 50% discount</h2>

<p>Both Hy4 models are priced per million tokens on OpenRouter, served from Tencent Cloud's first-party endpoint. Both carry a standing 50% discount right now, and the list price is what the discount is applied to.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Input / 1M</th><th>Output / 1M</th><th>Cached read / 1M</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hy4 flash (promo)</td><td>$0.035</td><td>$0.1405</td><td>$0.0015</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 flash (list)</td><td>$0.07</td><td>$0.281</td><td>$0.003</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 (promo)</td><td>$0.417</td><td>$1.2505</td><td>$0.021</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 (list)</td><td>$0.834</td><td>$2.501</td><td>$0.042</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 preview</td><td>$0.834</td><td>$2.501</td><td>$0.042</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>OpenRouter labels the Tencent Cloud endpoints <strong>50% off</strong>, and the arithmetic is exact on every line: $0.07 divided by two is $0.035, $0.281 divided by two is $0.1405, $0.003 divided by two is $0.0015. The same holds for the flagship — $0.834 halved is $0.417, $2.501 halved is $1.2505.</p>

<p>Two things to flag before you plan around that discount. First, <strong>neither OpenRouter nor the Tencent model card states an end date</strong> for the promotion — there is no "until December 31" language anywhere in either source. An open-ended discount is a fact today, not a commitment, and any budget built on the promotional rate should have a fallback to the list rate. Second, the two rates differ by 12x on input, so which one you are quoted changes the shape of the whole model comparison.</p>

<p>There is no free tier for either model. OpenRouter's live catalog carries twelve free-tier models in total and <strong>none of them are Hy4</strong>.</p>

<h2 id="comparisons">What $0.035 buys you against the alternatives</h2>

<p>Here is Hy4 flash's promotional input rate against the cheapest 1M-context, image-capable models in OpenRouter's catalog of 477 models. Prices are the live per-million rates; output is in the second column.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Input / 1M</th><th>Output / 1M</th><th>Relative to Hy4 flash</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>GLM-5.3 Flash (batch)</td><td>$0.023</td><td>$0.068</td><td>cheaper on both</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Hy4 flash</strong></td><td><strong>$0.035</strong></td><td><strong>$0.1405</strong></td><td>baseline</td></tr>
<tr><td>DeepSeek-V4.1 Flash (batch)</td><td>$0.035</td><td>$0.149</td><td>same input, ~6% more on output</td></tr>
<tr><td>GLM-5.3 Flash</td><td>$0.0399</td><td>$0.0784</td><td>14% more on input, 44% less on output</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claude Haiku 5.5</td><td>$0.10</td><td>$0.50</td><td>2.9x input, 3.6x output</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 4 Hydrogen</td><td>$1.50</td><td>$7.50</td><td>43x input, 53x output</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemini 4 Neon</td><td>$3.00</td><td>$15.00</td><td>86x input, 107x output</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The single most useful comparison is with <strong>Claude Haiku 5.5</strong>, and it is not a close one. On the list price, Hy4 flash is <strong>30% cheaper on input</strong> ($0.07 versus $0.10) and <strong>44% cheaper on output</strong> ($0.281 versus $0.50), with cached reads at $0.003 against $0.010 — 70% cheaper. On the promotional rate it is less than half Haiku's input price.</p>

<p>That matters because Tencent's own blind evaluation put Hy4 flash within a rounding error of Haiku 5.5 on user satisfaction: <strong>2.79 out of 4</strong> against Haiku 5.5's <strong>2.81 out of 4</strong>, across 259 tasks and 85 internal users. If those two numbers are close enough, the price gap is the tiebreaker, and a <strong>median 6.7-minute end-to-end completion time at RMB 0.26 per task</strong> was the fastest in that evaluation. A vendor-reported internal benchmark is not third-party verification, so treat the satisfaction score as directional — but the price gap is measured from live rate cards.</p>

<p>Against Google's own frontier, Hy4 flash is two orders of magnitude cheaper. Gemini 4 Neon costs $3.00 input and $15.00 output; a request with one million input and one million output tokens costs <strong>$18.00 on Neon</strong> and <strong>$0.351 on Hy4 flash at list price</strong>, or $0.1755 on the promotional rate. That is <strong>51x the Hy4 flash list rate</strong> and 103x its promotional rate on the same workload. Nobody should read that as "Hy4 flash is as good as Neon" — it is not, and the license and the limitations below matter. It means that for high-volume, long-context extraction, summarization, and document work, the model you reach for first should not be your most expensive one.</p>

<h2 id="family">Hy4 versus Hy4 flash: 770B or 220B</h2>

<p>The two models are not cut-down versions of each other; they are different architectures sharing a name.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Hy4</th><th>Hy4 flash</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Total parameters</td><td>770B</td><td>220B (incl. 64B Engram)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active per token</td><td>49B + 1.23B vision</td><td>12B + 0.43B vision</td></tr>
<tr><td>Layers</td><td>78 (1 dense + 77 MoE)</td><td>42 (1 dense + 41 MoE)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Experts per MoE layer</td><td>256 routed + 1 shared</td><td>512 routed + 1 shared</td></tr>
<tr><td>Active routed experts</td><td>8</td><td>16</td></tr>
<tr><td>Attention</td><td>Gated DeepSeek Sparse Attention + IndexCache</td><td>Hybrid sparse + sliding-window (1:3)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Speculative drafter</td><td>DFly2, 3.3B</td><td>DFly2, 0.85B</td></tr>
<tr><td>Context</td><td>1,048,576</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr>
<tr><td>Output tokens (max)</td><td>65,536</td><td>65,536</td></tr>
<tr><td>Modalities</td><td>text + image in, text out</td><td>text + image in, text out</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Hy4 flash is cheaper for three reasons that are stated in its own model card: a much smaller backbone, a <strong>LatentMoE</strong> design where routed experts operate in a lower-dimensional latent space, and an <strong>Engram</strong> layer of learned bigram and trigram embeddings retrieved through hash lookups. Its hybrid attention and latent experts bring the KV cache down to <strong>one twenty-fourth of Hy3 and one-sixth of Hy4</strong>, which is what makes a 1M-token context window affordable to serve.</p>

<p>Tencent's own recommendation is the practical summary: use <strong>Hy4 flash for most everyday productivity work</strong> — deep search, document processing, slide creation, simple front-end demos, computer use — and <strong>Hy4 for long-horizon software engineering</strong> where the higher intelligence ceiling earns its price. On Tencent's internal WorkIndex benchmark, Hy4 flash scores 42.1 against Hy4 preview's 39.7 at <strong>one thirteenth the cost per task</strong>, and one twentieth of Hy4's.</p>

<h2 id="license">The license changed, and Section 10 is the part to read</h2>

<p>This is the section that most write-ups skipped, and it is the one that can cost you a legal review.</p>

<p>Hy4 preview's model card says it is released under Apache License 2.0, and its LICENSE file is a permissive statement of that. Hy4 and Hy4 flash ship under a different document: the <strong>Tencent Hy License, Version 1.0</strong>, described in its own header as "derived from the Apache License, Version 2.0, with modifications, mainly the additional commercial terms for large Model-as-a-Service businesses in Section 10."</p>

<p>The license defines MaaS as "giving third parties remote access to model inference or fine-tuning (e.g., via API)," and explicitly carves out two cases: end-user products whose model capabilities are confined to specific features or workflows, and products that merely relay requests to an independently operated third-party model service.</p>

<p>Then Section 10.2 sets the threshold. If you operate a MaaS business, you must obtain a <strong>separate written license from Tencent</strong> in either of two cases:</p>

<ul>
<li>your aggregate worldwide gross revenue exceeds <strong>US$50 million</strong> in any consecutive 12-month period — whether or not that revenue came from the model; or</li>
<li>you use the model in or for a product or service with more than <strong>100 million monthly active users</strong> in any calendar month.</li>
</ul>

<p>Section 10.3 exempts purely internal use, provided neither the work nor its outputs are made available to third parties. Section 10.4 gives the contact address: <code>hy_partners@tencent.com</code>.</p>

<p>Read that against your own situation carefully. An internal team serving its own applications is clearly covered by the internal-use exemption. A platform reselling inference built on Hy4 weights is not. A product with 120 million users that ships a Hy4-powered feature is not — the 100 million MAU test applies to the product, not to the model usage. And the $50 million test is on gross revenue, not on revenue attributable to the model, so a large company using Hy4 for a trivial internal feature is caught by 10.2(a) even though the model contributes nothing to that revenue.</p>

<p>This is a materially different posture from the Apache 2.0 terms most open-weight buyers assume they are getting. It is not a prohibition — the license is usable, and Tencent publishes the address to negotiate — but it is a term that belongs in your legal review rather than in a footnote.</p>

<h2 id="capabilities">Capabilities, and what the model card says it cannot do</h2>

<p>Both models take text and images and produce text. Both support tool calling, structured outputs, reasoning modes, and a 1M-token context. Beyond that the two diverge sharply, and Tencent is unusually explicit about the gaps.</p>

<p>Hy4's own <strong>Known Limitations</strong> section states that a notable gap remains between it and top proprietary models, "particularly on challenging internal benchmarks for research-level mathematics, long-horizon coding, model self-improvement, and advanced visual question answering." Hy4 preview's card is blunter still: it names "spending longer than necessary reasoning through complex tasks" and "a tendency to over-verify its own work" as known issues shipped with the release.</p>

<p>Hy4 flash's card adds the two things that make it interesting to serve: post-training reinforcement learning <strong>cuts average end-to-end output tokens by 50%</strong>, and combining megakernels, 4-bit quantization, and the DFly2 speculative drafter pushes decoding speed <strong>past 150 tokens per second</strong>. Halving output tokens is a token-efficiency win, not a price win — worth knowing if you are comparing against a model that emits twice as many tokens for the same answer, because it changes the output column of any cost comparison in a way that has nothing to do with the rate card.</p>

<p>The DFly2 drafter is worth understanding if you self-host: each draft layer shares the target model's KV cache and the indexer's selected top-2048 positions, so a draft token attends to at most ~2K keys regardless of context length, no separate KV cache is maintained, and the prefill-to-decode transfer adds only one hidden state. That is what makes long-context speculative decoding affordable.</p>

<h2 id="hosting">What self-hosting actually costs</h2>

<p>If you would rather run the weights yourself, the download sizes are the number that decides it.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Download size</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hy4 (BF16)</td><td>1,548.9 GB</td><td>294 files, 770B params</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 (FP8)</td><td>786.3 GB</td><td>quantized instruct release</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 flash (BF16)</td><td>440.5 GB</td><td>200 files</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 flash (FP8)</td><td>287.1 GB</td><td>the serveable release</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 flash (GGUF Q4_K_M)</td><td>168.3 GB</td><td>via AngelSlim, for local use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hy4 (GGUF Q4_K_M)</td><td>470.2 GB</td><td>via AngelSlim</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Hy4 flash's FP8 weights are 287 GB, which fits comfortably on eight 80 GB H100s and on sixteen 48 GB cards. The official vLLM image is <code>vllm/vllm-openai:hy4-flash</code>, launched with <code>--tensor-parallel-size 8</code> and <code>--speculative-config '{"num_speculative_tokens":5,"method":"dfly"}'</code>; SGLang ships a matching <code>lmsysorg/sglang:hy4-flash</code> image with <code>--speculative-algorithm DFLY</code>.</p>

<p>For comparison, Thinking Machines' Inkling 2 Mini — the other open-weight model that landed in November 2026 — is a <strong>single 20 GB checkpoint</strong> in mixed NVFP4 and MXFP8, runnable on a 32 GB Apple Silicon Mac or a DGX Spark. The gap between a model you can download before dinner and one that needs a rack is the gap between "open weight" as a demo and "open weight" as an operating model. Hy4 flash is firmly in the second category.</p>

<h2 id="code">Calling Hy4 flash in 30 seconds</h2>

<p>Both models expose an OpenAI-compatible endpoint, so the SDK change is a base URL and a model slug.</p>

<pre><code>curl https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/chat/completions \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_OPENROUTER_API_KEY" \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "model": "tencent/hy4-flash",
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Summarize this quarterly report and flag any figure that changed by more than 20%."
      }
    ],
    "max_tokens": 1024
  }'</code></pre>

<p>And the same call from Python, with the reasoning mode set explicitly — which matters, because the model card's recommended sampling is <code>temperature=0.9</code>, <code>top_p=1.0</code>, and the default reasoning effort is the deep chain-of-thought mode.</p>

<pre><code>from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI(
    base_url="https://openrouter.ai/api/v1",
    api_key="YOUR_OPENROUTER_API_KEY",
)

response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="tencent/hy4-flash",
    messages=[
        {"role": "user", "content": "Summarize this quarterly report and flag any figure that changed by more than 20%."}
    ],
    temperature=0.9,
    top_p=1.0,
    max_tokens=1024,
    extra_body={
        "chat_template_kwargs": {"reasoning_effort": "no_think"},
    },
)

print(response.choices[0].message.content)</code></pre>

<p>If you self-host with vLLM instead, the client is identical and the base URL points at your own server:</p>

<pre><code>from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI(base_url="http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1", api_key="EMPTY")

response = client.chat.completions.create(
    model="hy4-flash",
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Describe this chart and list the three largest values."}],
    temperature=0.9,
    top_p=1.0,
    extra_body={"chat_template_kwargs": {"reasoning_effort": "no_think"}},
)
print(response.choices[0].message.content)</code></pre>

<p>The reasoning switch matters more than it looks. The model card's recommendation for direct responses is to pass <code>reasoning_effort: "no_think"</code>, and because reinforcement learning cut average output tokens by 50%, leaving the default deep reasoning mode on for high-volume traffic is the single easiest way to give back most of the price advantage you came for.</p>

<h2 id="caveats">Limits worth knowing before you ship</h2>

<p><strong>Single provider, no fallback.</strong> Hy4 flash is served on OpenRouter by exactly one provider — Tencent Cloud — with 99.76% uptime over the last day and 100% over the last 30 minutes. Hy4 has two: Tencent Cloud plus Together. If you need a failover path, Hy4 has one today and Hy4 flash does not.</p>

<p><strong>No EU in-region routing.</strong> Both model pages state that they are "not currently available through EU in-region routing," which is a data-residency consideration for European deployments.</p>

<p><strong>Output ceiling of 65,536 tokens</strong> on both models, against a 1M context window. Long-context reading is fine; very long generation is capped.</p>

<p><strong>Reasoning effort differs from the sibling models.</strong> The OpenRouter endpoint lists <code>reasoning</code>, <code>include_reasoning</code>, <code>response_format</code>, <code>structured_outputs</code>, <code>tools</code>, and <code>tool_choice</code> as supported on Hy4 flash. The Together-hosted Hy4 endpoint additionally supports <code>top_p</code>, <code>top_k</code>, <code>logprobs</code>, <code>seed</code>, <code>min_p</code>, and the usual penalty knobs — so sampling parameters available on one endpoint are not guaranteed on another.</p>

<p><strong>No free tier</strong> on either model, and no free variant on any aggregator.</p>

<p>For readers who want a second source, <a href="https://deepinfra.com/?ref=apirank" class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">DeepInfra</a> serves <code>tencent/Hy4-preview</code> on its own OpenAI-compatible API at <strong>$0.834 input and $2.501 output per million</strong>, which is the same rate OpenRouter lists for the preview checkpoint, and it lists the flagship's 1,048,576-token context with a cached-read rate of $0.042 per million. It is a genuine second provider rather than a reseller of the same endpoint, which matters because Hy4 flash has only one.</p>

<p><strong>Tencent's blind evaluation is vendor-reported.</strong> The satisfaction score, the median completion time, and the RMB 0.26 cost per task all come from Tencent's own internal users. They are directional indicators, not an independent benchmark.</p>

<h2 id="verdict">Verdict</h2>

<p>Hy4 flash is a genuinely interesting release: a native-multimodal 1M-context MoE at $0.035 per million input tokens, with output tokens cut by half through training rather than through a cheap model, a KV cache small enough to serve long contexts economically, and a self-host path that works if you have the rack space.</p>

<p>Use it for high-volume document and long-context work, agent pipelines, code assistance, and routine automation where a Claude Haiku 5.5-class model would be overkill on price. Budget against the <strong>list</strong> rate of $0.07 rather than the promotional $0.035, because nothing in either primary source commits to the discount lasting.</p>

<p>And if you are building a served product rather than an internal tool, read Section 10 before you ship. The Tencent Hy License is usable and negotiable, but it is not the Apache 2.0 that Hy4 preview shipped under, and the threshold that decides whether you need a written agreement with Tencent is a revenue test and a user-count test — not a use-case test. The model card's headline is the price. The license is what needs a lawyer.</p>

